     The ssbbuuff__pprriinnttff() function formats its arguments according to the format
     string pointed to by _f_m_t and appends the resulting string to the _s_b_u_f at
     the current position.

     The ssbbuuff__vvpprriinnttff() function behaves the same as ssbbuuff__pprriinnttff() except that
     the arguments are obtained from the variable-length argument list _a_p.

     The ssbbuuff__ppuuttcc() function appends the character _c to the _s_b_u_f at the cur‐
     rent position.

     The ssbbuuff__ttrriimm() function removes trailing whitespace from the _s_b_u_f.

     The ssbbuuff__eerrrroorr() function returns any error value that the _s_b_u_f may have
     accumulated, either from the drain function, or ENOMEM if the _s_b_u_f over‐
     flowed.  This function is generally not needed and instead the error code
     from ssbbuuff__ffiinniisshh() is the preferred way to discover whether an sbuf had
     an error.

     The ssbbuuff__ffiinniisshh() function will call the attached drain function if one
     exists until all the data in the _s_b_u_f is flushed.  If there is no at‐
     tached drain, ssbbuuff__ffiinniisshh() NUL-terminates the _s_b_u_f.  In either case it
     marks the _s_b_u_f as finished, which means that it may no longer be modified
     using ssbbuuff__sseettppooss(), ssbbuuff__ccaatt(), ssbbuuff__ccppyy(), ssbbuuff__pprriinnttff() or
     ssbbuuff__ppuuttcc(), until ssbbuuff__cclleeaarr() is used to reset the sbuf.

     The ssbbuuff__ddaattaa() function returns the actual string; ssbbuuff__ddaattaa() only
     works on a finished _s_b_u_f.  The ssbbuuff__lleenn() function returns the length of
     the string.  For an _s_b_u_f with an attached drain, ssbbuuff__lleenn() returns the
     length of the un-drained data.  ssbbuuff__ddoonnee() returns non-zero if the _s_b_u_f
     is finished.

     The ssbbuuff__ssttaarrtt__sseeccttiioonn() and ssbbuuff__eenndd__sseeccttiioonn() functions may be used for
     automatic section alignment.  The arguments _p_a_d and _c specify the padding
     size and a character used for padding.  The arguments _o_l_d___l_e_n_p and
     _o_l_d___l_e_n are to save and restore the current section length when nested
     sections are used.  For the top level section NULL and -1 can be speci‐
     fied for _o_l_d___l_e_n_p and _o_l_d___l_e_n respectively.

     The ssbbuuff__hheexxdduummpp() function prints an array of bytes to the supplied
     sbuf, along with an ASCII representation of the bytes if possible.  See
     the hexdump(3) man page for more details on the interface.

     The ssbbuuff__pprriinnttff__ddrraaiinn() function is a drain function that will call
     printf, or log to the console.  The argument _a_r_g must be either NULL, or
     a valid pointer to a _s_i_z_e___t.  If _a_r_g is not NULL, the total bytes drained
     will be added to the value pointed to by _a_r_g.

     The ssbbuuff__ppuuttbbuuff() function printfs the sbuf to stdout if in userland, and
     to the console and log if in the kernel.  The _s_b_u_f must be finished be‐
     fore calling ssbbuuff__ppuuttbbuuff().  It does not drain the buffer or update any
     pointers.

NNOOTTEESS
     If an operation caused an _s_b_u_f to overflow, most subsequent operations on
     it will fail until the _s_b_u_f is finished using ssbbuuff__ffiinniisshh() or reset us‐
     ing ssbbuuff__cclleeaarr(), or its position is reset to a value between 0 and one
     less than the size of its storage buffer using ssbbuuff__sseettppooss(), or it is
     reinitialized to a sufficiently short string using ssbbuuff__ccppyy().

     Drains in user-space will not always function as indicated.  While the
     drain function will be called immediately on overflow from the _s_b_u_f___p_u_t_c,
     _s_b_u_f___b_c_a_t, _s_b_u_f___c_a_t functions, _s_b_u_f___p_r_i_n_t_f and _s_b_u_f___v_p_r_i_n_t_f currently
     have no way to determine whether there will be an overflow until after it
     occurs, and cannot do a partial expansion of the format string.  Thus
     when using libsbuf the buffer may be extended to allow completion of a
     single printf call, even though a drain is attached.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The ssbbuuff__nneeww() function returns NULL if it failed to allocate a storage
     buffer, and a pointer to the new _s_b_u_f otherwise.

     The ssbbuuff__sseettppooss() function returns -1 if _p_o_s was invalid, and zero other‐
     wise.

     The ssbbuuff__bbccaatt(), ssbbuuff__ccaatt(), ssbbuuff__ccppyy(), ssbbuuff__pprriinnttff(), ssbbuuff__ppuuttcc(), and
     ssbbuuff__ttrriimm() functions all return -1 if the buffer overflowed, and zero
     otherwise.

     The ssbbuuff__eerrrroorr() function returns a non-zero value if the buffer has an
     overflow or drain error, and zero otherwise.

     The ssbbuuff__lleenn() function returns -1 if the buffer overflowed.

     The ssbbuuff__ccooppyyiinn() function returns -1 if copying string from userland
     failed, and number of bytes copied otherwise.

     The ssbbuuff__eenndd__sseeccttiioonn() function returns the section length or -1 if the
     buffer has an error.

     The ssbbuuff__ffiinniisshh(_9) function (the kernel version) returns ENOMEM if the
     sbuf overflowed before being finished, or returns the error code from the
     drain if one is attached.

     The ssbbuuff__ffiinniisshh(_3) function (the userland version) will return zero for
     success and -1 and set errno on error.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     #include <sys/types.h>
     #include <sys/sbuf.h>

     struct sbuf *sb;

     sb = sbuf_new_auto();
     sbuf_cat(sb, "Customers found:\n");
     TAILQ_FOREACH(foo, &foolist, list) {
             sbuf_printf(sb, "   %4d %s\n", foo->index, foo->name);
             sbuf_printf(sb, "      Address: %s\n", foo->address);
             sbuf_printf(sb, "      Zip: %s\n", foo->zipcode);
     }
     if (sbuf_finish(sb) != 0) /* Check for any and all errors */
             err(1, "Could not generate message");
     transmit_msg(sbuf_data(sb), sbuf_len(sb));
     sbuf_delete(sb);

